package org.eclipse.rdf4j.sail.lmdb.evaluation;

import org.eclipse.rdf4j.common.annotation.Experimental;

/**
 * A closed interval of estimated work, used to compare execution strategies without pretending to a precision the
 * cardinality estimator does not have.
 * <p>
 * <b>Unit.</b> One work-row is the cost of the interpreted nested-loop path touching one already-located quad. A random
 * index seek costs {@link #SEEK} work-rows. Every strategy is described in these units, so a strategy that touches
 * fewer rows and one that touches the same rows more cheaply can be compared on the same scale.
 * <p>
 * <b>Why an interval and not a number.</b> {@link SlotPlan#estimate} answers {@link Double#POSITIVE_INFINITY} when it
 * has no idea, which is indistinguishable from "astronomically expensive" — and treating "unknown" as "expensive" would
 * make any plan containing a filter or an OPTIONAL lose every comparison it entered. {@link #UNKNOWN} is
 * <code>[0, +inf)</code> instead: it is consistent with any cost, so it neither {@linkplain #beats dominates} anything
 * nor is dominated by anything, and a strategy nobody can price simply keeps whatever position it already had.
 * <p>
 * <b>Arbitration rule.</b> Selection is by strict domination — a candidate wins on cost only when its whole interval
 * lies below every rival's whole interval ({@link #beats}). When no candidate dominates, cost has not decided and the
 * caller falls back to its declared preference order. The consequence is the property that makes this safe to roll out
 * across a whole benchmark corpus at once: a miscalibrated model produces wide or overlapping intervals, nothing
 * dominates, and dispatch behaves exactly as it did before.
 */
